    "available_at": "2026-11-03T03:13:20.639220+00:00",
    "body": "Federal and state law enforcement agencies arrested more than 230 undocumented immigrants, including more than 40 commercial driver’s license holders, during a 72-hour enforcement operation spanning 15 states.\nThe operation, dubbed Operation Guardian , was conducted Oct. 26-28 by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) and state and local law enforcement partners, according to the U.S. Department of Homeland Security.\nIt targeted immigrants DHS described as holding unauthorized CDLs, along with other individuals the agency identified as public safety threats connected to the trucking industry.\nOperation Guardian follows recent truck blitzes in Arkansas , Arizona, California, Kentucky and North Dakota .\nDHS Secretary Markwayne Mullin announced the results Monday during a news conference at Melton Truck Lines’ headquarters in Tulsa, Oklahoma.\nThe operation involved authorities in Alabama, Arkansas, Florida, Georgia, Indiana, Iowa, Louisiana, Mississippi, Missouri, Nebraska, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee and Texas.\nDHS did not provide a state-by-state breakdown of Operation Guardian arrests or identify which states accounted for the more than 40 CDL holders detained.\nThe announcement also did not specify which states issued the licenses, the number of commercial vehicles inspected or how many CDL holders were actively operating trucks when apprehended.\nOperation Guardian follows another federal immigration enforcement initiative targeting commercial vehicles.\nOn Sept. 25, ICE launched Operation Guardrail, a nationwide effort targeting commercial vehicles operating in violation of U.S. laws.\nAs of Oct. 29, ICE had arrested 303 people through Operation Guardrail, according to DHS.\nThe agency also reported that between Jan. 20, 2025, and Oct. 28, 2026, ICE made 4,795 CDL-related arrests nationwide, including 1,233 arrests involving CDL holders.\nHowever, DHS did not explain whether the latest Operation Guardian arrests were included in its cumulative figures or whether any individuals arrested were charged with licensing fraud or other CDL-related offenses.\nThe agency also did not release information establishing how many of the detained CDL holders had obtained their licenses through fraudulent applications or were otherwise ineligible to hold them.\nWhy it matters: The coordinated crackdown signals escalating federal and state scrutiny of commercial drivers’ immigration status and CDL eligibility.",
